End-Stage Heart Failure
Palliative & End of Life
Dyspnea at rest, Refractory edema/anasarca, Cardiac cachexia

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: PEL-026
Reason for Visit: Acute escalation of severe orthopnea and paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnea rendering the patient unable to sleep or lie flat.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient, spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported the patient awoke suddenly with severe paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnea and extreme air hunger, resulting in panic and near-syncope when attempting to mobilize to a chair. Urgent after-hours assessment was required to acutely manage the severe fluid overload, relieve the critical respiratory distress, and prevent an immediate, catastrophic respiratory failure prior to morning.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I'm drowning. I can't catch a breath unless I sit perfectly straight, and my legs are so heavy they feel like they will burst."
History of Present Illness: Over the past two weeks, the patient has experienced relentless progression of severe, disabling dyspnea explicitly present at rest or with minimal physical exertion. Tonight, the patient suffered an episode of paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnea causing sudden, distressing awakenings with severe air hunger. The spouse reports intractable orthopnea strictly necessitating sleeping in an upright, seated position. The patient has refractory peripheral edema relentlessly progressing to anasarca, accompanied by ascites leading to profound abdominal distension and persistent early satiety. There is chronic, debilitating fatigue and generalized physical weakness. The patient has a history of recurrent, frequent hospitalizations for acute cardiac decompensation. The spouse notes cardiac cachexia presenting as involuntary, severe, and progressive muscle wasting over the upper body.
Functional Status: Bed/chair bound; entirely dependent on others for all transfers and personal care due to extreme fatigue and breathlessness.
Social/Safety Concerns: Caregiver exhaustion from continuous overnight monitoring; extremely high risk of skin breakdown due to severe anasarca.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 95/60 mmHg | HR: 118 bpm | Temp: 36.4 C | RR: 28 bpm | SpO2: 89% on room air | Wt: 82 Kg (significant rapid fluid weight gain)
General: Visibly cachectic upper body with grossly edematous lower body, sitting bolt upright, tachypneic, and diaphoretic.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular exam reveals cool, mottled extremities objectively indicating poor peripheral perfusion, and a visibly elevated JVP to the angle of the jaw. Respiratory auscultation demonstrates bilateral coarse basal crackles extending to the mid-zones. Abdomen is distended with positive shifting dullness. The diagnostic tetrad is fulfilled by the observed dyspnea at rest (RR 28, inability to speak in full sentences), refractory edema/anasarca (massive 4+ pitting edema to the sacrum), and profound cardiac cachexia (temporal and clavicular wasting).
Environmental Observations: The patient is sleeping in a standard recliner; multiple pillows are improperly stacked, failing to provide adequate truncal support.
Medication Reconciliation: The patient has been compliant with high-dose oral furosemide, which is now completely failing to induce diuresis due to poor gut absorption from bowel edema.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: End-Stage Heart Failure - Status: Exacerbated (Acute Decompensation)
Differentials:

Acute pulmonary embolism - Ruled out/unlikely given the insidious 2-week build-up of massive fluid overload and absence of unilateral calf pain or sudden pleuritic chest pain.

COPD exacerbation - Ruled out/unlikely due to the absence of wheeze, the presence of massive anasarca, and the classic orthopneic presentation.

Severe sepsis - Ruled out/unlikely due to the absence of fever, rigors, or localized source of infection.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient is in acute, critical extremis due to end-stage heart failure complicated by severe right and left ventricular decompensation. The objective findings of profound anasarca, hypoxia, and bilateral pulmonary crackles indicate catastrophic fluid overload. Oral loop diuretics are entirely failing due to gut edema. Delaying medical assessment until regular clinic hours would definitively result in fatal pulmonary edema or acute cardiogenic shock. Immediate after-hours intervention was absolutely necessary to administer potent parenteral diuretics to offload the fluid and parenteral opioids to rapidly alleviate the terrifying air hunger and reduce cardiac preload.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered stat intravenous furosemide to rapidly initiate diuresis and offload the pulmonary circulation. Administered stat subcutaneous morphine to reduce the central perception of breathlessness, decrease extreme anxiety, and provide mild venodilation.
Positive Behavioral Change: When you feel the need to sleep (trigger), you must firmly use the electric controls to elevate the head of the bed to at least 45 degrees before closing your eyes (behavior). This uses gravity to keep the fluid in your legs rather than allowing it to pool in your lungs, directly preventing those terrifying waking episodes of breathlessness (positive consequence).
Home-Based Services (Optional): Urgent referral to the community heart failure/palliative team for daily IV or subcutaneous diuretic management.
DME/Safety (Optional): Urgent provision of a hospital bed with electric profiling and a pressure-relieving mattress.
Follow-up: Community nursing review in 12 hours to assess urine output and respiratory status.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If the patient becomes completely unarousable, develops severe central chest pain radiating to the jaw, or if the breathing becomes so compromised that the patient turns blue and gasps continuously despite sitting fully upright, the spouse must call 000 immediately for emergency transfer.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The heart muscle is irreversibly failing. The goal is purely symptom relief, reducing the suffocating fluid, and preventing agonizing distress.
Clinical Course: The ability of the kidneys to respond to diuretics will continue to wane. Palliative focus is definitively required moving forward.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Spouse: Ensure the patient remains strictly sitting upright. Do not offer any oral fluids tonight. Monitor for excessive drowsiness following the morphine injection.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your heart is too weak to pump the blood forward, so fluid is backing up into your lungs and legs. Because your stomach is swollen, your oral water pills are no longer absorbing. We gave the diuretic straight into your vein to force your kidneys to pee out the fluid and gave a small injection of morphine to instantly take away the terrifying feeling of suffocation.
Actionable Steps: Remain sitting up. Use the bedside urinal to avoid the extreme exertion of walking to the bathroom. Restrict your fluid intake strictly to the amount discussed tomorrow.

